# WSauthor: worldshaper
# A sealed room with nothing in it that gives off light. The answer is black.
#
# This exists to hold one clause honestly: **if a surface receives no light it is black, and there
# is no minimum anywhere**. That is easy to assert and easy to lose — a constant added to an ambient
# term reads as taste, survives every other test, and only shows up as a room that will not go dark.
# Two of them lived in `resolve.comp` until R9 measured the light they were standing in for
# (`kIndirectFloor`, which was half of the sky term wherever the sky could not be seen, and
# `kGroundBounce`, which was added to every surface in the world unconditionally).
#
# Nothing here is subtle: a box, hollowed, with no opening, no emitter and no sky. The sun cannot
# reach it, the sky cannot reach it, there is no lamp in the list, and every gathering ray inside it
# lands on a surface that is itself receiving nothing. Every term is nought by construction, so the
# picture is a measurement of whether the renderer agrees.

metre 32
bounds -4 -0.5 -4   4 3.5 4

material darkstone rgb=170,166,160 rough=210

# Two metres of wall on every side, so nothing leaks in through a corner and no ray finds its way
# out through the shared edge of two cells — which a voxel grid lets a ray do (see `face_point` in
# shaders/shade_faces.comp), and which would put a little sky in here and make this test pass for
# the wrong reason.
let shell = box -4 -0.5 -4   4 3.5 4
let void  = box -2  0.5 -2   2 2.5 2
let walls = difference { shell void }

paint darkstone

solid walls

# The player stands in the middle of the void: 4 m by 2 m by 4 m of air, two metres of stone around
# it on every side.
origin 0 -1.5 0
